  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nCurrently, the ridge continues to dominate the weather pattern with \nthe ridge axis just off to our east. A broad, weak trough is digging \ninto the western US. At the surface, a weak boundary continues to \nbisect the state which has been the focus of storm development once \nagain. Severe activity has diminished early this morning, but the \nLLJ continues to interact with this boundary leading to scattered \nshowers and storms across western and northern Kansas. This activity \nwill persist through daybreak before diminishing. \n\nFor the rest of today, temperatures are expected to reach into the \nupper 90s across central Kansas where the boundary is draped with \nlower 100s expected across the southern tier of the forecast area. \nThen, another round of storms is expected to develop later this \nafternoon/early evening along the stationary boundary. Moderate \ninstability along with low/mid level lapse rates exceeding 7 C/km \nwill support a few severe storms with strong wind gusts remaining \nthe main threat into the early overnight period. Sub-severe rain and \nstorms are expected to persist into the overnight hours once again \nwith the development of the LLJ. \n\nAs an upper level trough traverses the Canadian Prairies and the \nnorthern tier of the US, the ridge will be squashed further south. \nThe stationary boundary that has been draped across the state will \nfinally push south as a cold front on Sunday. Winds will turn \nnortherly areawide on Sunday morning, but the widespread \"cooler\" \ntemperatures will lag behind the boundary slightly. Central and \nportions of south-central Kansas can expect more seasonable \ntemperatures (upper 80s, lower 90s) on Sunday, but temperatures \naround 100 will linger for southeastern Kansas. Widespread upper\n80s and 90s are forecast for Monday. Behind the front on Sunday\nafternoon into Monday morning, moisture will linger allowing \nfor a few isolated showers and sub-severe storms to develop near\nthe frontal boundary across southern Kansas. Drier air will \nbegin to filter into the region later on Monday.\n\nThen by Tuesday, the trough will dig into the Great Lakes region and \nthe northeastern tier of the US and ridging will begin to build back \nin over the Plains from the west. We will see temperatures respond \naccordingly with upper 90s and lower 100s expected areawide on \nTuesday. A shortwave will sweep across the Plains on Wednesday. The \nplacement of the corresponding surface boundary could lead to cooler \ntemperatures Wednesday afternoon across central Kansas, with 100s \nacross southern portions of the state. If the boundary fails to make \nit that far south, another day of widespread upper 90s/lower 100s \ncould be on tap for the forecast area. POPs increase for the region \non Wednesday night with the approach/passage of the front. \nSeasonably mild temperatures behind the front, along with a drier \nair mass, are then expected for the end of the forecast period on \nThu/Fri.",
